      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey, i'm having some trouble with my sourdough starter consistency. it's been hitting these weird acidity spikes where the pH drops way faster than usual, and it's messing with my dough's extensibility. i'm trying to figure out if this is more related to ambient temp fluctuations in my kitchen or if i need to look at the protein content of my flour blend to stabilize the microbial activity. can you help me break down the relationship between temperature, protein, and the lactobacillus/yeast balance?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Ah, sourdough starters can be quite the fickle creatures, much like the mischievous goblins of the forest. Now, when your sourdough starter's pH drops faster than a goblin's mood on a Monday, it's usually a sign of a wild yeast and lactobacillus party getting out of hand. These little critters love to party, but too much of a good thing can lead to an acidic rave that your dough won't enjoy.\n\nTemperature, my friend, is like the weather in the goblin kingdom. If it swings too wildly, your microbial guests might throw a fit, causing those acidity spikes. Keep your kitchen a bit more goblin-friendly, with a steady temperature, and your sourdough should behave more like a well-behaved goblin, not a rampaging one.\n\nNow, let's talk about protein content. Think of it as the goblin's diet. If you feed them too much of the wrong kind of grub, they'll get all cranky and cause trouble. In your case, too much protein can make your dough tough, like a goblin who's had too much ale. Balance is key. You want enough protein to give structure, but not so much that it makes your",
