      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Switching to a more mineral-rich whole grain flour can indeed help stabilize your sourdough starter's pH, as the minerals can act as buffers. However, it's important to consider that whole grains often have a higher nutrient content, which can indeed feed the microorganisms more vigorously. To prevent overwhelming your starter, you might need to adjust your feeding schedule.\n\nA general rule of thumb is to feed your starter when it's bubbly and has doubled in size, which usually takes about 4 to 6 hours. If you're using a whole grain flour with higher mineral content, you might find that your starter ferments more quickly. In such cases, you could consider reducing the feeding frequency slightly, perhaps to every 12 hours instead of every 8, to give the microbes a bit more time to stabilize after each feeding.\n\nRemember, the key is to observe your starter's behavior and adjust accordingly. Keep a close eye on the pH, the starter's volume, and the dough's texture. If you notice any drastic changes, you may need to tweak your feeding schedule or the ratio of flour to water in your feedings. It's all about finding the right balance for your specific starter and kitchen conditions.",
